# SLEEP(3)

## NAME

sleep - suspend execution for interval

## SYNOPSIS

```c
unsigned sleep(unsigned seconds);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The current process is suspended from execution for the number of seconds specified by the
argument. The actual suspension time may be up to 1 second less than that requested, because
scheduled wakeups occur at fixed 1-second intervals, and an arbitrary amount longer because of
other activity in the system.

The routine is implemented by setting an alarm clock signal and pausing until it occurs. The
previous state of this signal is saved and restored. If the sleep time exceeds the time to the
alarm signal, the process sleeps only until the signal would have occurred, and the signal is
sent 1 second later.

**Note:** it is declared by no header - v7 had none - so a caller declares it itself.

## SEE ALSO

alarm(2), pause(2), setjmp(3)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/sleep.c

## BESM-6 NOTES

The handler this routine installs leaves by longjmp(3) and never returns through the signal
frame at all, which makes *sleep* the one place in libc that exercises a non-local exit from a
handler. It is the last leg of lib/test/signals, and the one test in the suite that takes a
second of wall clock.

It is also why setjmp(3) keeps a slot for the mode register that the calling convention would
otherwise make unnecessary: a handler is not a call boundary.
